@@ -0,0 +1,99 @@
+.. _deadletter:
+
+SQLite terminal-failure archive
+-------------------------------
+
+Enable an independent, durable inventory of failed invocations::
+
+    from huey import SqliteHuey
+
+    huey = SqliteHuey('app', filename='app.db', failure_archive=True)
+
+    @huey.task(retries=2)
+    def index_record(record_id):
+        raise ValueError('index unavailable')
+
+    index_record(42)
+    # The consumer executes the initial attempt and any retries.
+
+    for failure in huey.failures(limit=100):
+        print(failure.failure_id, failure.task_id, failure.error)
+        print(failure.traceback)
+        receipt = huey.redrive(failure.failure_id, retries=1, delay=30)
+        print(receipt.task_id)  # A new invocation, with a new identity.
+
+The default is disabled and preserves the ordinary SQLite schema. Enabling
+requires an on-disk SQLite backend. With ``create_tables=False``, call
+``huey.storage.initialize_schema()`` explicitly. If immediate mode uses memory
+debug storage, initialize the database through a normal SQLite instance first
+when automatic creation is disabled. Queued/scheduled work, existing results
+and unrelated application tables remain intact.
+
+``FailureRecord`` and ``RedriveReceipt`` are exported by
+``huey.contrib.deadletter``. Records expose a stable string ``failure_id``, an
+increasing integer ``cursor``, original ``task_id`` and registered ``name``,
+``error``, ``traceback``, UTC Unix ``failed_at``, serialized invocation ``data``,
+``parent_failure_id``, ``redriven`` and ``replacement_id``. These are snapshots.
+Decode ``data`` with ``huey.deserialize_task`` when task functions are registered;
+reading metadata alone does not require registration.
+
+``failures(limit=100, after=None)`` lists records in ascending cursor order,
+strictly after a supplied cursor. ``limit`` must be a positive integer;
+``after`` must be a nonnegative integer or ``None``. Booleans are invalid.
+``failure(failure_id)`` reads one record, or returns ``None``. Both reads are
+non-destructive. ``delete_failure(failure_id)`` returns whether one record was
+deleted. It does not remove queued replacements, ordinary results, other
+records or lineage links. Deleted cursors are not reused. All operations are
+scoped to the Huey queue name and raise ``ConfigurationError`` when disabled.
+
+An invocation is frozen before pre-execute hooks and task code run. Only the
+terminal attempt is archived, retaining its initial arguments, options,
+callbacks and retry/backoff state even if execution mutates them. The archive
+is independent of ``results``, intermediate error results, result consumption
+and ``storage.flush_results()``. Recording precedes post-execute hooks and
+error callbacks. Serialization or archival failures propagate to the caller
+of ``execute``; consumers retain their normal error-reporting behavior.
+
+Terminal ordinary exceptions, exhausted timeouts, locks and non-retrying rate
+limits are archived. Intermediate retries, successful recovery, ``RetryTask``,
+``CancelExecution`` (including retrying cancellation), revoked/expired tasks,
+pre-execute cancellation, interrupted execution and periodic invocations are
+excluded. Existing executor callbacks, signals and chord outcomes still run.
+
+``redrive(failure_id, retries=0, delay=None, priority=None)`` creates one new root
+message. Missing failures raise ``KeyError``. ``retries`` is a nonnegative
+integer; ``delay`` is a finite nonnegative number of seconds or ``None``;
+``priority`` is a finite number or ``None``. Booleans are invalid. Validation
+precedes mutations. A valid repeated call returns the original committed
+receipt, even with different options; the first committed options win.
+
+The root and every success/error callback receive fresh unique IDs. Old chord
+bindings are removed throughout those chains; the callback graph reachable
+only through a chord binding is not replayed. Ordinary attached pipelines still
+run, but replay cannot count another completion in an old chord. Root retries
+and optional priority are overridden; other invocation options are preserved.
+Old ETA values are cleared, and a positive delay sets a new root ETA. Relative
+expiry is renewed from redrive time on the root and normal enqueue time on
+callbacks. Absolute expiration deadlines are explicitly disabled, including
+class-level defaults, so they do not suppress deliberately replayed work.
+
+The receipt exposes ``failure_id``, new root ``task_id``, all fresh ``task_ids``
+in root/success/error traversal order, and ``result``. Result reading uses the
+ordinary root ``Result`` or success-pipeline ``ResultGroup`` shape, or ``None``
+if results are disabled. Receipt handles do not retain rescheduling arguments.
+Immediate mode keeps normal calls unchanged; redrive always queues to SQLite,
+and receipt reads use that durable broker. It does not execute replay inline.
+
+Queue insertion, the one-time receipt and every lineage link commit in one
+SQLite transaction. Concurrent redrivers obtain one identity. Failed insertions
+or process death before commit leave the record unclaimed and no replacement.
+After commit the receipt remains reserved even when the work is consumed. A
+failed replay or its callbacks create distinct failures pointing to the parent
+that generated that replay graph. This survives retries, restart and deliberate
+deletion of the parent record. Lineage links are retained independently.
+
+This is an operator replay protocol, not worker acknowledgement or malformed
+message quarantine. It does not guarantee capture before a killed process has
+committed its failure, exactly-once external effects, or atomicity across the
+whole executor. Existing delivery and configured SQLite power-loss guarantees
+remain unchanged. Applications own deliberate archive retention and deletion.
